---
title: Frangipane Mince Pies
yield: 24
course: Dessert
locale: en
difficulty: Medium
cuisine: British
---

= Pastry =

Make the pastry by putting the @plain flour{200%g}, @ground almonds{50%g}, @icing sugar{50%g}, and @butter{125%g}(chilled and diced), in a food processor. Pulse until the mixture resembles fine breadcrumbs and starts to clump together a little. Add the @egg yolk{1} and pulse again, then gradually add 1–2 tbsp of @very cold water{2%tbsp}, until the mixture comes together into a slightly tacky dough. Wrap in cling film and chill.

= Frangipane =

To make the frangipane, beat the @butter{130%g}(softened) and @caster sugar{130%g} together until very soft and aerated. Add the @eggs{3}, @ground almonds{150%g}, @plain flour{25%g}, @almond extract{a few drops}, and @salt{pinch}. Mix to combine.

= Assemble =

Preheat the oven to 180°C.

To assemble, roll out the pastry to around 3 mm thick on a lightly floured work surface. Cut out 24 rounds using a 7.5–8 cm cutter, and line two 12-hole fairy cake tins (or one 12-hole deep round botttom tins). Add a heaped teaspoon of @mincemeat{600%g} to each round of pastry, followed by a generously heaped teaspoon of the frangipane. Use a knife or spatula to spread the frangipane evenly over the mincemeat, adding a little more if necessary to seal round the edges, making sure the mincemeat is completely covered. Sprinkle with @flaked almonds{50%g}.

Bake in the preheated oven for 15 to ~{17%minutes}, until the pastry is crisp and the frangipane is lightly browned and slightly domed.

Leave the mince pies to cool in the tins. They freeze very well and will keep for up to a week in an airtight container. Serve with a dollop of @./Sweet/Rum Butter{}.